WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.500
Two differentiable update maps share one parameter theta.
The initial state is fixed; the loss depends only on the final state.

00:03.500 --> 00:08.800
The blue forward route evaluates x0, then x1, then x2,
and finally the scalar loss L.

00:08.800 --> 00:14.200
The terminal loss seeds lambda2. Orange arrows apply transposed
state Jacobians to obtain lambda1 and lambda0 in reverse order.

00:14.200 --> 00:16.900
A is the local state Jacobian. B is the local parameter Jacobian.
Reverse accumulation multiplies each next adjoint by A transpose.

00:16.900 --> 00:22.900
The total parameter gradient adds both local contributions:
B0 transpose lambda1 plus B1 transpose lambda2.

00:22.900 --> 00:27.700
This is a gradient. An optimiser chooses a separate update.
Other initial-state or direct-loss dependence requires extra terms.
